{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>\"You know that my wife bore me two sons.\"</b> If one were to say that he is unique to his mother, what is the sense in this, that his gray hair would go down in sorrow to Sheol after him, given that he has many sons and grandsons, and his mother is already dead and would not weep over him to his face? Rather, the reason is that Jacob never took a wife of his own will except Rachel, and this is the sense of \"my wife bore me\" — for only two were born to me from a woman who is my wife by my will, and I have set my love upon them as though they were my only ones, while the rest are to me like sons of concubines. \"And his brother is dead,\" and this one alone is my unique son whom I love. Therefore Scripture gives precedence to Rachel over Leah: \"like Rachel and like Leah who both built the house of Israel\" (Ruth 4:11), \"and he called to Rachel and to Leah the field\" (Genesis 31:4), for she is foremost in his thought. And the commentators said that for this reason it states in this passage (Genesis 46:19), \"the sons of Rachel, Jacob's wife,\" for she is truly called his wife without deception. But in my opinion this was because he mentioned her there among the maidservants, and I have already noted this (Ramban on Genesis 31:33)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {0 "Ramban's cross-reference is to his own commentary on Genesis 31:33."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:51.853900332Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}